# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Luke'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'gospels'
SUBTITLE = 'The Son of David, Good Tidings to Israel, and the Things Written Concerning Him'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Luke is written “in order” that the reader “mightest know the certainty of those things, wherein thou hast been instructed” (Luke 1:1, 4). It tells of the promise that the Lord God shall give the Son “the throne of his father David,” the good tidings of a Saviour born in the city of David, the Lord’s preaching “the acceptable year of the Lord,” the road to Jerusalem, the cup of the new testament, and the risen Lord opening the things written “in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me” (Luke 1:32–33; 2:10–11; 4:18–19; 22:20; 24:44).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'The portions of Luke treated here belong to the Prophecy Program: the promise to Mary speaks of the throne of David and the house of Jacob, and the Lord promises the Twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel (Luke 1:32–33; 22:29–30). Its words on mercy and love are also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Luke', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Promise and the Birth (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'The promise of the throne of David and the house of Jacob, and the good tidings to the shepherds (Luke 1:32–33, 68; 2:10–11).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Anointed Preacher (Chs. 3–4)'), ('p', 'The Lord reads from the prophet and preaches the acceptable year of the Lord and the kingdom of God (Luke 4:18–19, 43).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Teaching and Mercy (Chs. 5–9)'), ('p', 'The Lord teaches love of enemies and mercy toward the unthankful (Luke 6:27, 31, 35–37).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Road to Jerusalem (Chs. 10–19)'), ('p', 'The lawyer’s question, the good Samaritan, the little flock and the Son of man come to seek and to save that which was lost (Luke 10:25–27, 37; 12:15, 32; 19:10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The Supper and the Cross (Chs. 20–23)'), ('p', 'The cup of the new testament and the kingdom appointed to the Twelve (Luke 22:20, 29–30).'), ('h3', 'Movement 6 — The Risen Lord (Ch. 24)'), ('p', 'The Lord expounds in all the scriptures the things concerning himself and opens the repentance and remission to be preached beginning at Jerusalem (Luke 24:25–27, 44, 47).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Luke', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Luke should be understood within Israel’s prophetic program. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants readers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Throne of David</b> — “The Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David” (Luke 1:32).', '<b>He Shall Reign Over the House of Jacob</b> — “He shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end” (Luke 1:33).', '<b>The Lord God of Israel</b> — “Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for he hath visited and redeemed his people” (Luke 1:68).', '<b>The Acceptable Year</b> — “To preach the acceptable year of the Lord” (Luke 4:19).', '<b>The Little Flock</b> — “Fear not, little flock; for it is your Father’s good pleasure to give you the kingdom” (Luke 12:32).', '<b>A Kingdom Appointed</b> — “I appoint unto you a kingdom… and sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel” (Luke 22:29–30).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Luke', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how He is presented. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how Luke presents Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Saviour, Christ the Lord</b> — “Unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord” (Luke 2:11).', '<b>Seeking the Lost</b> — “The Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost” (Luke 19:10).', '<b>The Cup of the New Testament</b> — “This cup is the new testament in my blood, which is shed for you” (Luke 22:20), reported as written, at the table with the Twelve.', '<b>Concerning Himself</b> — “Beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded unto them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself” (Luke 24:27).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Luke', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Luke speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Luke operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Luke in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Luke reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the throne of David and the house of Jacob (Luke 1:32–33)', 'the Lord God of Israel who visited and redeemed his people (Luke 1:68)', 'the little flock given the kingdom (Luke 12:32)', 'the twelve tribes of Israel judged from thrones (Luke 22:30)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Luke that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Luke contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Throne of David — “the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David.” (Luke 1:32).', 'The House of Jacob — “he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever.” (Luke 1:33).', 'Redeemed His People — “Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for he hath visited and redeemed his people.” (Luke 1:68).', 'The City of David — “unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.” (Luke 2:11).', 'The Acceptable Year — “To preach the acceptable year of the Lord.” (Luke 4:19).', 'The Kingdom to the Little Flock — “it is your Father’s good pleasure to give you the kingdom.” (Luke 12:32).', 'Seeking the Lost — “the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost.” (Luke 19:10).', 'The Cup — “This cup is the new testament in my blood, which is shed for you.” (Luke 22:20).', 'A Kingdom Appointed — “I appoint unto you a kingdom.” (Luke 22:29).', 'Twelve Tribes Judged — “sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel.” (Luke 22:30).', 'The Things Written — “all things must be fulfilled, which were written in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me.” (Luke 24:44).', 'Beginning at Jerusalem — “that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in his name among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem.” (Luke 24:47).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Luke Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Luke and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on the prophetic setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Luke teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Luke', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Throne of David', '“the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 1:32'], ['The House of Jacob', '“he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 1:33'], ['Redeemed His People', '“Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for he hath visited and redeemed his people.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 1:68'], ['The City of David', '“unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 2:11'], ['The Acceptable Year', '“To preach the acceptable year of the Lord.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 4:19'], ['The Kingdom to the Little Flock', '“it is your Father’s good pleasure to give you the kingdom.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 12:32'], ['Seeking the Lost', '“the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 19:10'], ['The Cup', '“This cup is the new testament in my blood, which is shed for you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 22:20'], ['A Kingdom Appointed', '“I appoint unto you a kingdom.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 22:29'], ['Twelve Tribes Judged', '“sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 22:30'], ['The Things Written', '“all things must be fulfilled, which were written in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 24:44'], ['Beginning at Jerusalem', '“that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in his name among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Luke 24:47']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Luke that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Luke teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Love Your Enemies', '“Love your enemies, do good to them which hate you.”', 'Luke 6:27'], ['As You Would Be Treated', '“as ye would that men should do to you, do ye also to them likewise.”', 'Luke 6:31'], ['Merciful as the Father', '“Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.”', 'Luke 6:36'], ['Love of God and Neighbour', '“Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart… and thy neighbour as thyself.”', 'Luke 10:27'], ['Go and Do Likewise', '“Go, and do thou likewise.”', 'Luke 10:37'], ['Beware Covetousness', '“a man’s life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth.”', 'Luke 12:15'], ['Humility', '“whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.”', 'Luke 14:11']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Luke without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s prophetic story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing Israel-program commands for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Luke in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Love your enemies</b> — Do good to them that hate you (Luke 6:27).', '<b>Be merciful</b> — As your Father is merciful (Luke 6:36).', '<b>Love God and neighbour</b> — With heart, soul, strength and mind (Luke 10:27).', '<b>Show mercy</b> — Go, and do likewise (Luke 10:37).', '<b>Beware covetousness</b> — Life is more than possessions (Luke 12:15).', '<b>Humble yourself</b> — He that humbleth himself shall be exalted (Luke 14:11).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Luke', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Luke in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Promise and the Birth (Chs. 1–2) — The throne of David. <i>(Luke 1:32–33, 68; 2:10–11)</i>', 'The Anointed Preacher (Chs. 3–4) — The acceptable year. <i>(Luke 4:18–19, 43)</i>', 'Teaching and Mercy (Chs. 5–9) — Love your enemies. <i>(Luke 6:27, 31, 36)</i>', 'The Road to Jerusalem (Chs. 10–19) — Seeking the lost. <i>(Luke 10:27, 37; 12:15, 32; 14:11; 19:10)</i>', 'The Supper and the Cross (Chs. 20–23) — A kingdom appointed. <i>(Luke 22:20, 29–30)</i>', 'The Risen Lord (Ch. 24) — The things concerning himself. <i>(Luke 24:25–27, 44, 47)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Luke, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward mercy, humility, and love of neighbour without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Luke simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Luke stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
